  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "id": "stadt-es-gibt",
      "title": "es gibt: there is, there are",
      "body": "**es gibt** means both *there is* and *there are*. It never changes.\n\nThe place after *es gibt* is the object, so it takes the accusative, just like after *ich möchte* and *ich habe*: *der* words take **einen**, *die* and *das* words don't change. Plurals have no *a*.\n\nTo ask, swap the two words and add **hier** for *around here*: **Gibt es hier** einen Park?",
      "table": [
        ["", "es gibt …"],
        ["der Park", "Es gibt einen Park."],
        ["die Bank", "Es gibt eine Bank."],
        ["das Kino", "Es gibt ein Kino."],
        ["die Kinos (plural)", "Es gibt zwei Kinos."]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stadt-plurals",
      "title": "Plurals of places",
      "body": "In the plural, the article is always **die**. Learn each noun's plural along with it; these patterns cover most of them.\n\nWatch out: *die Bank* (the bank) becomes **die Banken**. *die Bänke* are benches!",
      "table": [
        ["Pattern", "Singular", "Plural"],
        ["-s", "das Kino", "die Kinos"],
        ["-n / -en", "die Straße", "die Straßen"],
        ["-e, often with ¨", "der Platz", "die Plätze"],
        ["-er with ¨", "das Haus", "die Häuser"],
        ["no ending", "das Theater", "die Theater"],
        ["-um → -en", "das Museum", "die Museen"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stadt-kein",
      "title": "kein: no, not a",
      "body": "**kein** means *no*, *not a* or *not any*. It takes the same endings as *ein*, so you just add a **k**.\n\nUnlike *ein*, **kein** also has a plural form: **keine**.\n\n- *Es gibt keinen Park.* There's no park.\n- *Ich habe keine Kinder.* I don't have any children.",
      "table": [
        ["", "a", "no / not a"],
        ["der word (Das ist …)", "ein Park", "kein Park"],
        ["der word (Es gibt …)", "einen Park", "keinen Park"],
        ["die word", "eine Bank", "keine Bank"],
        ["das word", "ein Kino", "kein Kino"],
        ["plural", "Kinos", "keine Kinos"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "stadt-kein-nicht",
      "title": "kein or nicht?",
      "body": "Both make a sentence negative, but they're used differently:\n\n- **kein** goes before a noun that would otherwise have *ein* or no article at all: *Das ist kein Hotel. Es gibt keine Cafés. Ich spreche kein Deutsch.*\n- **nicht** is for everything else: verbs, words like *weit* and *hier*, and nouns with *der, die, das*: *Das ist nicht weit. Ich wohne nicht hier. Das ist nicht das Hotel.*\n\nGerman has no word for *don't*: *I don't live here* is simply *Ich wohne nicht hier.*"
    },
    {
      "id": "stadt-directions",
      "title": "Asking the way",
      "body": "Asking:\n\n- **Entschuldigung, wo ist …?** Excuse me, where is …?\n- **Gibt es hier …?** Is there … around here?\n- **Ich suche …** I'm looking for …\n- **Ist das weit?** Is it far?\n\nAnswers:\n\n- **links** left, **rechts** right\n- **geradeaus** straight ahead, **immer geradeaus** keep going straight ahead\n- **da** there, **da links** there on the left\n- **nicht weit** not far, **nah** close"
    }
  ],
